{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"d23e ROUND 9 = PASS (0 blocking, 9 advisory) after NINE rounds. SIGNED + CERTIFIED at 2b5bb00c, CLAIMED, IMPLEMENTED, COMMITTED (18ecd9709), PUSHED as Gerrit change 1164. Awaiting LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1.\n\nEVIDENCE THE WORK IS SOUND, not just green:\n* Full unit suite: 6374 passed, 19 skipped, 1 failed in 13m24s — and the single failure was tests/unit/test_check_docs_index.py::test_current_repo_index_is_clean, which I had PREDICTED and FIXED mid-run (the suite read the pre-fix state). Re-ran it alone: 13 passed. That gate requires every top-level docs/*.md to be linked from docs/README.md, so my new docs/llm-example-configs.md would have failed CI. I found it by reading the gate rather than waiting for it.\n* MUTATION CHECK, 4/4 BITE. M1 move the read back INSIDE the dict comprehension -> the exactly-one-warning test fails at 3 warnings/call (observed for real during implementation, not simulated). M2 delete the config.py warning -> config-path test fails. M3 flip _scheduled to _permanent -> registration test fails. M4, THE VALUABLE ONE, put the warning in resolve_model_string (the rejected placement) -> the NEGATIVE CONTROL fails, which proves that control earns its place: it is the test that forbids warning on an already-resolved string where provenance is lost. Every mutation restored by saving content in-process — NEVER `git checkout`, which would have destroyed uncommitted work.\n* lint clean, mypy clean on 407 source files, docs-index checker exit 0, env-registry drift gate exit 0. config.py is 651 LOC — under the 700 policy target the b300 seam test asserts, which is exactly the headroom b300 was extracted to create, so this ticket landed the change b300 existed to make landable.\n\nNEW CAVEAT FOR MUTATION TESTING, learned here: after restoring identical-size content immediately following a mutation, a test can FAIL on STALE BYTECODE — .pyc invalidation keys on source mtime AND size, and a same-size rapid rewrite can defeat it. My restore-verification compared file contents and correctly reported 'restored cleanly: True' while the very next pytest run failed. I nearly read that as a real regression. Clear __pycache__ before trusting a post-mutation run.\n\nALSO learned the hard way: I broke the test file into 113 syntax errors by editing at a LINE NUMBER that my own earlier edit had shifted, clobbering an assertion message with a docstring opener. Match on CONTENT, never on an index, after you have already edited a file."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785592965801565001,"uuid":"acaac4e5-daf4-4e05-adea-ede00aaaeaf2"}